  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Express $[\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta -\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ]^{6}$ in the form $x+\\text{i}y,$ where $x$ and $y$ are functions of $\\theta .$ Use $i$ to denote the imaginary unit.\n\n$[\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta -\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ]^{6}=$\n\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "For expressions involving subtraction, De Moivre's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{[\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(\\phi )]}^{n}=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(n\\phi )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(n\\phi ).\n$$\n\nIn our expression, we have $n=6$ and $\\phi =\\theta .$ Substituting in these values, we get\n\n$$\n[\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\theta -\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\theta ]^{6}=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(6\\theta )-\\text{i}\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(6\\theta ).\n$$",
